data.content:
Since there's no `.csproj` visible, here are two options ranked lightest-first:

---

### Option 1 — `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+, zero new types)

.NET 8 ships an abstract `TimeProvider` class. Accept it via constructor, default to `TimeProvider.System`, and in tests pass `FakeTimeProvider` (from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`).

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider clock)
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock = clock;
    public InvoiceCalculator() : this(TimeProvider.System) { }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

Test:
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Option 2 — Tiny `IClock` interface (any .NET version)

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }
public sealed class SystemClock : IClock {
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same constructor-default pattern; tests supply a stub.

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, go with `TimeProvider` — it's built-in, already understood by `Task.Delay`/`PeriodicTimer`, and Microsoft ships a test fake. Want me to apply either option to your code?
